     "title": "THC in cannabis may reduce nightmares in people with PTSD",
     "url": "https://www.newscientist.com/article/2584744-thc-in-cannabis-may-reduce-nightmares-in-people-with-ptsd/?utm_campaign=RSS|NSNS&utm_content=home&utm_medium=RSS&utm_source=NSNS",
     "time": "08-14 22:02",
     "ts": 1786716154,
     "summary": "A drug made up of THC, the main psychoactive component of cannabis, reduced the severity and incidence of nightmares in people with PTSD",
     "source": "New Scientist",
     "content": "The main psychoactive component of cannabis seems to reduce persistent and distressing nightmares in people with post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). The drug dronabinol – purified tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) oil extracted from cannabis plants – was more than twice as likely to lead to a complete cessation of nightmares in people with the mental health condition than a placebo. Although it is unclear why it has this effect, THC may suppress the stage of sleep when dreams are most likely to occur.\nAbout 4 per cent of the global population have PTSD at some point in their lives. It causes people to repeatedly recollect a traumatic event, often through nightmares. Treatments like trauma therapy can help, but these aren’t always easily accessible, and no interventions specifically focus on relieving nightmares.\nStefan Röpke at the Charité–Universitätsmedizin Berlin hospital in Germany sees people with PTSD every day and says some report getting relief from their nightmares if they take a small amount of cannabis before bed. “That was the rationale [for the trial],” he says.\nAdvertisement\nRöpke and his colleagues recruited 171 people with PTSD who experienced frequent, intense nightmares, assessed via a standard 0-to-8-point scale. Eighty-seven of the participants were instructed to take dronabinol – which is approved to treat nausea and vomiting caused by cancer drugs, and loss of appetite in people with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome (AIDS) – 1 hour before bed every night for 10 weeks. The remaining 84 participants took a placebo.\nThey started on 2.5 milligrams and gradually increased the dose until their nightmares stopped or they reached the maximum 15 mg dose. Röpke stresses people shouldn’t self-medicate with cannabis: “Treatment with dronabinol [and similar interventions] should be prescribed and monitored by a physician.”\nAfter 10 weeks, nightmare severity decreased by about 3.7 points in the dronabinol group – 1.5 points more than the placebo group. “A 4-point improvement could mean having nightmares several times a week with considerable distress at baseline and having only occasional, mildly distressing nightmares after treatment,” says Röpke.\n“Based on the moderate effect size for the nightmares outcome, I would say the findings are clinically relevant,” says Carrie Cuttler at Washington State University.\nThe researchers also found that 58 per cent of the dronabinol group experienced at least a 50 per cent reduction in their nightmare frequency and intensity, compared with 29 per cent of the placebo group. What’s more, 37 per cent in the former group experienced a complete cessation of their nightmares, compared with 15 per cent of the placebo group. “What impressed us most was the magnitude of the effect,” says Röpke.\nTHC’s psychoactive effects meant that around 4 in 5 of the participants who took it gauged that they weren’t in the placebo group. However, half of those given a placebo thought they received dronabinol. Expectancy of a",
     "_extract_method": "trafilatura",
     "zh": "大麻中的THC或可缓解PTSD患者的噩梦",
     "content_zh": "大麻主要精神活性成分THC似乎能缓解创伤后应激障碍（PTSD）患者的持续性痛苦噩梦。从大麻提取的THC油药物屈大麻酚，让PTSD患者完全停止噩梦的概率是安慰剂的两倍以上，其作用机制可能是抑制梦境多发的睡眠阶段。全球约4%的人一生中会患PTSD，该疾病会让人反复因噩梦回忆创伤事件，现有创伤治疗不一定能便捷获取且无专门针对噩梦的干预手段。研究者已招募171名频繁做噩梦的PTSD患者开展相关试验。"
